@startuml char_device_io
!theme plain
title Character Device I/O Flow

participant Userspace
participant VFS as "VFS Layer"
participant CharDev as "cdev\n(file_operations)"
participant Driver
participant Hardware

== Write Path ==
Userspace -> VFS: write("/dev/mydev", buf, len)
VFS -> CharDev: Call .write()
CharDev -> Driver: my_write()
Driver -> Driver: copy_from_user(buf)
Driver -> Hardware: Write to memory-mapped\nregister or DMA buffer
CharDev --> VFS: return bytes_written
VFS --> Userspace: return bytes_written

== Read Path ==
Userspace -> VFS: read("/dev/mydev", buf, len)
VFS -> CharDev: Call .read()
CharDev -> Driver: my_read()
Driver -> Hardware: Read from memory-mapped\nregister or DMA buffer
Driver -> Driver: copy_to_user(buf)
CharDev --> VFS: return bytes_read
VFS --> Userspace: return bytes_read

== ioctl Path ==
Userspace -> VFS: ioctl("/dev/mydev", cmd, arg)
VFS -> CharDev: Call .unlocked_ioctl()
CharDev -> Driver: my_ioctl(cmd, arg)
Driver -> Driver: Validate command
Driver -> Hardware: Hardware-specific operation
Driver -> Driver: copy_to_user() if needed
CharDev --> VFS: return 0
VFS --> Userspace: return 0

note right of Driver
  Must handle:
  - User/kernel buffer copy
  - Permission checks
  - Error codes (EFAULT, EINVAL, etc)
  - Locking (spinlock for quick ops)
end note

@enduml
